    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That \"goblin eyes\" thing is a funny way to put it, but I get the point about contrast. \n\nRegarding the screen reader part, I'm specifically worried about the more complex interactive elements like the transaction history tables and the data visualization charts. How do I make sure those don't become a mess for screen reader users?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The interactive elements can be a bit of a \"goblin's playground\" when it comes to accessibility, can't they? But don't worry, I've got some tips to help you tame the beast.\n\nFor transaction history tables and data visualization charts, you'll want to make sure that screen readers can accurately read the content. Here are a few things to check:\n\nFirst, ensure that the table headers and rows are labeled correctly using ARIA attributes like `role=\"heading\"` and `role=\"row\"`. This will help screen readers understand the structure of the table and provide a clear reading experience.\n\nSecond, use a consistent naming convention for your data visualization elements, such as using `aria-label` attributes to describe the chart or graph. This will help screen readers understand what's being displayed and provide a clear narrative.\n\nThird, consider using a screen reader-friendly chart library or plugin that can help simplify the reading experience. Some popular options include Chart.js and Highcharts, which offer built-in support for screen reader accessibility.\n\nLastly, don't forget to test your app thoroughly with different screen readers and devices to ensure that the interactive elements are working as expected. It's like navigating a dark cave, where you need to rely on your other senses to",
      "source": "target",
